WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.970
-Our first guest tonight is an
Academy Award-Winning actress

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:05.905
you know from her work
in films such as

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:08.674
"Vicky Cristina Barcelona,"
"Volver," and "Nine."

00:00:08.674 --> 00:00:12.077
She's received her fourth Oscar
nomination for her performance

00:00:12.077 --> 00:00:13.212
in "Parallel Mothers,"

00:00:13.212 --> 00:00:14.814
which is in theaters
and On Demand now.

00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:15.815
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:18.284 line:0%
[ Speaking Spanish ]

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:47.513
Please welcome to the show,
Penelope Cruz, everyone.

00:00:47.513 --> 00:00:49.916
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:52.452
Hello, Penelope. Welcome.
-Hi.

00:00:52.452 --> 00:00:53.620
How are you?

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.623
-I'm wonderful.
It's so lovely to see you.

00:00:56.623 --> 00:00:58.757
-Great to see you, too.
Thank you so much.

00:00:58.757 --> 00:00:59.925
-So, this is so exciting.

00:00:59.925 --> 00:01:01.927
Not only is it your fourth
Oscar nomination,

00:01:01.927 --> 00:01:05.131
but your husband, Javier Bardem,
also got nominated for an Oscar.

00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:07.833
I'm wondering, on the day,
were your children --

00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:09.769
how do they react to the news
that both of their parents

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:12.171
are Oscar-nominated?

00:01:12.171 --> 00:01:16.876
-Well, they found out later,
because when we saw them,

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:21.414
they -- when we saw it live,
it was 2:30 P.M. here

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:22.681
and they were in the school.

00:01:22.681 --> 00:01:25.117
And then when we told them
later, the only question

00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:28.988
that my daughter asked me
was if there was going

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:30.823
to be any cake to celebrate.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:30.823 --> 00:01:32.325
That was the only comment,

00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:34.594
and I think that's a very
healthy reaction.

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:36.095
[ Laughter ]

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:38.898
-How long -- how long
was the gap between

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:41.033
when one of you found out
you were nominated

00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:43.970
and the other one
found out they were nominated?

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:47.439
-It was, like,
probably like five minutes.

00:01:47.439 --> 00:01:50.710
I mean, you know, a huge
surprise to hear one name,

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:55.615
then to hear the other one, too,
is really,

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:57.450
really something to celebrate.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.819
A part of me still
doesn't believe it.

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:01.921
-This is also --
must be extra special.

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:05.091
This is your seventh film
with Pedro Almodovar,

00:02:05.091 --> 00:02:10.329
who you first worked with when
you were a teenager, correct?

00:02:10.329 --> 00:02:13.232
-Yeah. I met him
when I was 18 or 19.

00:02:13.232 --> 00:02:15.568
He called me when he saw
my first two movies.

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:17.369
And when he called me,
I thought it was a joke,

00:02:17.369 --> 00:02:20.506
because it was my dream
in my childhood

00:02:20.506 --> 00:02:23.409
and my teenager years
to meet him,

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:25.811
to thank him for all the work
that he did

00:02:25.811 --> 00:02:27.747
and maybe one day
to work with him.

00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:30.049
So, yeah, when he called me,

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:32.818
it was like an instant
connection.

00:02:32.818 --> 00:02:36.022
We felt like already
we knew each other,

00:02:36.022 --> 00:02:39.191
and we decided to keep
working together,

00:02:39.191 --> 00:02:40.893
and we've done seven movies
together.

00:02:40.893 --> 00:02:42.395
I hope many more.

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:45.264
-And this is a story,
"Parallel" -- "Parallel Mothers"

00:02:45.264 --> 00:02:50.403
is based on a story that he told
you about decades ago, correct?

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:54.640
-Yeah, he told me the first time
maybe like 20 years ago,

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:57.242
when we were doing press
for "All About My Mother,"

00:02:57.242 --> 00:03:00.580
and then he never mentioned the
story again for so many years

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:05.084
And in the middle of the first
lockdown -- COVID lockdown --

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:07.286
he called me and he said,
"I'm writing this for you,

00:03:07.286 --> 00:03:08.487
and this is the story."

00:03:08.487 --> 00:03:10.523
And I told him
that I remembered every detail

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:13.826
because he shared with me
so long before.

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:18.097
-And your first film with him,
is it true that you did a scene

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:22.368
with your future mother-in-law?

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:25.471
Yes, yes, with Pilar Bardem.

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:29.074
It was kind of a rehearsal
of real life.

00:03:29.074 --> 00:03:31.477
I mean, thank God I didn't have
to give birth in a bus.

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:34.079
Like, that's what happened
with my character in the film.

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:39.319
But, yeah, yeah, destiny had
like a lot of plans for us.

00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:41.220
It was a my first movie
with Pedro,

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:42.488
and it was the opening
of the film.

00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:45.491
It was like only 10 minutes
of the film,

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:50.430
and I was giving birth in a bus
for those 10 minutes.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.030
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:54.767
-Pedro really likes using you
as a mother character.

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:56.235
I think that that's safe to say.

00:03:56.235 --> 00:04:00.139
-Yes, he always saw my very
strong maternal instincts.

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:01.741
-Yes.
-And all the movies

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:03.776
that I've done with him,
I was either pregnant

00:04:03.776 --> 00:04:05.878
or I was a mother,
except for one movie

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:07.780
called "Broken Embraces."

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.284
All the other films,
he has really taken

00:04:11.284 --> 00:04:14.420
advantage of that
maternal instinct.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:16.989
-And now, your mother
was a hairdresser.

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:19.458
And I'm wondering, did you ever
pick up any of those skills?

00:04:19.458 --> 00:04:21.394
Did they come in handy
during this pandemic,

00:04:21.394 --> 00:04:24.797
when none of us could go
get our hair done?

00:04:24.797 --> 00:04:26.732
-Well, that's funny
that you say that,

00:04:26.732 --> 00:04:29.201
because I'm always looking
for victims, you know?

00:04:29.201 --> 00:04:33.405
Like, people that let me cut
their hair or do waxing

00:04:33.405 --> 00:04:35.641
or all the things that I learned
there with my mother,

00:04:35.641 --> 00:04:38.911
but I always try
to cut people's hair, always.

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:42.081
And, yeah, it's true
that during those times,

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:45.785
I cut everybody's hair,
so I was happy about that.

00:04:45.785 --> 00:04:48.520
And a lot of friends of mine,
like my friend Salma Hayek,

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:51.257
she has trusted me doing
her hair and makeup,

00:04:51.257 --> 00:04:53.426
waxing, all kinds of things,
so...

00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:54.860
-Well, you must be good at it.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:57.730
Has anyone ever -- I mean,
even though it's a free haircut,

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:00.099
has anyone ever demanded
their money back?

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:02.067
-No, they haven't.
They haven't complained.

00:05:02.067 --> 00:05:03.636
I mean, you can try once.

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:06.005
If you want,
you're more than welcome.

00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:09.408
-Sign me up.
-[Indistinct]

00:05:09.408 --> 00:05:12.211
-I gave Javier a hard time
the last time he was here.

00:05:12.211 --> 00:05:13.412
I didn't really give him
a hard time.

00:05:13.412 --> 00:05:15.314
I said one of my favorite
haircuts in any movie

00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:16.682
is his in "The Counselor."

00:05:16.682 --> 00:05:19.118
And I asked him what you felt

00:05:19.118 --> 00:05:21.119
about it when he had
this hairstyle.

00:05:21.119 --> 00:05:23.356
Will you tell us what your take
was when you saw?

00:05:23.356 --> 00:05:24.991
[ Laughter ]

00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:27.360
-I was kind of used to it
already,

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.930
because I've seen things
like, uh,

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.999
"No Country for Old Men," like,
you know...

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:34.100
-Yeah.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.068
I guess that --
"No Country" might be

00:05:36.068 --> 00:05:40.039
the least-sexy haircut
in any film, ever.

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:42.442
-Yeah, but he was so great
in the film,

00:05:42.442 --> 00:05:47.379
and, yeah, that was a really
strange character

00:05:47.379 --> 00:05:49.848
to the one in "The Counselor,"
but he was great also.

00:05:49.848 --> 00:05:51.350
He's always so good.
He's such a great actor.

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:53.552
-You were both great
in that film.

00:05:53.552 --> 00:05:56.955
I want to ask, you know,
in these final days,

00:05:56.955 --> 00:05:59.959
is it a stressful time,
or is it just exciting

00:05:59.959 --> 00:06:02.862
to be nominated for an Oscar?

00:06:02.862 --> 00:06:07.467
-It's just exciting,
and it just makes me think about

00:06:07.467 --> 00:06:08.968
all the people
that have been there,

00:06:08.968 --> 00:06:10.202
you know,
from the beginning.

00:06:10.202 --> 00:06:12.471
Obviously, about Pedro,
because he has trusted me

00:06:12.471 --> 00:06:14.474
so much from day one,
and he gives me

00:06:14.474 --> 00:06:16.542
all these incredible characters.

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:18.711
When I hear my name in a
nomination like this,

00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:20.412
and it's the first time
I think --

00:06:20.412 --> 00:06:23.382
I feel very grateful
for my peers,

00:06:23.382 --> 00:06:25.217
my colleagues, the people
that are voting for me,

00:06:25.217 --> 00:06:29.388
and for all the people
that keep giving me, you know,

00:06:29.388 --> 00:06:32.791
great characters and good
films and the opportunity

00:06:32.791 --> 00:06:36.795
to make a living out of this
profession, out of this job.

00:06:36.795 --> 00:06:40.766
It was my dream all my life,
growing up, to be an actor,

00:06:40.766 --> 00:06:42.568
and when I said it
to my parents,

00:06:42.568 --> 00:06:44.937
it sounded like I wanted
to go to the moon

00:06:44.937 --> 00:06:48.140
because we didn't know anybody
in our neighborhood

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:50.542
or family or friends,
anybody that could make

00:06:50.542 --> 00:06:54.313
a living out of anything
related to the arts.

00:06:54.313 --> 00:06:57.750
So it just makes me think
of all of them.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:00.252
Also about my parents,
that they didn't laugh at me

00:07:00.252 --> 00:07:03.956
when I said to them,
"I want to be an actress."

00:07:03.956 --> 00:07:07.392
So, yeah, I feel very, very
grateful, very grateful.

00:07:07.392 --> 00:07:08.794
-You're very lucky
to have that support,

00:07:08.794 --> 00:07:10.329
and it really is
a beautiful film.

00:07:10.329 --> 00:07:11.863
I really want everybody
to see it.

00:07:11.863 --> 00:07:13.365
I don't want to give anything
away talking about it,

00:07:13.365 --> 00:07:15.000
because it is really
something to see.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.334
It's well-earned.

00:07:16.334 --> 00:07:18.037
Congratulations, and thanks
so much for being here.

00:07:18.037 --> 00:07:19.504
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:19.504 --> 00:07:21.173
-Thank you.
Thank you so much.

00:07:21.173 --> 00:07:22.508
-Penelope Cruz, everybody.

00:07:22.508 --> 00:07:25.144
"Parallel Mothers" is in
theaters and On Demand now.

